             rxg_reload - reload '.rxg' files (all racks)

Syntax:    rxg_reload

Response:  rxg_reload/rxg,chan,freq,pol,file,type,lo1,lo2,year,month,day,pol1,dpfu1,pol2,dpfu2

Monitor-only Parameters:

  rxg    literal text, 'rxg', to indicate this line has '.rxg' file
         information for the LO 'chan'
  chan   loX, where X is one of:
            1, 2, or 3 for Mark III/IV/5 and K-4,
            a, b, c, or d for VLBA/4/5
            1, 2, 3, or 4 for LBA/4,
            a, b, c, d, 2a, 2b, 2c, or 2d for DBBC
            a0, a1, b0, b1, c0, c1, d0, or d1 for RDBE
            a, b, c, d, e, f, g, or h for DBBC3
            1-8 for all others.
  freq   LO frequency for this channel, MHz.
  pol    Polarization for this channel, unknown, rcp, or lcp.
  file   name of the '.rxg' file that matched the LO value; 'undefined' if
         there was no match, in that case the remaining fields are omitted
  type   LO type from '.rxg' file: 'fixed' or 'range'
  lo1    first LO value from '.rxg' file, MHz
  lo2    second LO value from '.rxg' file, if present, MHz
  year   the year from the date in the '.rxg' file
  month  the month from the date in the '.rxg' file
  day    the day from the date in the '.rxg' file
  pol1   first polarization from the '.rxg' file
  dpfu1  DPFU of the first polarization in the '.rxg' file
  pol2   second polarization from the '.rxg' file, if present
  dpfu2  DPFU of the second polarization in the '.rxg' file, if present

Comments:
This command attempts to read the '.rxg' files in the
'/usr2/control/rxg_files' directory. It is useful for updating the '.rxg' file
information in the FS without restarting. (Note that the directory may be a
symbolic link that may have been changed since the FS started.) It is
recommended to only use this command when there is no active schedule and the
FS is quiescent.

If there is no error, the "rxg summary response" for each currently defined LO
is displayed and the contents of each referenced '.rxg' file is recorded in
the log file (but not displayed). All previous '.rxg' file information in the
FS is removed/replaced. The result is the same as if the FS was restarted.

If there is an error in reading any of the files, all the new '.rxg' file
information is discarded. All previous '.rxg' file information in the FS is
retained. The result is the same as if the 'rxg_reload' command had not been
used.

It may happen that an '.rxg' that is not currently needed has a error that is
preventing an urgently needed '.rxg' from being loaded. In that case, the bad
file can temporarily be renamed to not end in '.rxg'. For example another
extension, like '.BAD' can be appended. The bad file should be repaired as
soon as possible, certainly before it is needed, its name restored, and
reloaded either with 'rxg_reload' or by restarting the FS.
